  "activity": {
    "id": "act_vsepr_walkthrough",
    "name": "Predicting Molecular Shape with VSEPR Theory",
    "description": "A guided AR exploration of VSEPR theory for General Chemistry I. Students learn to count electron domains from a Lewis structure, watch electron domains settle into minimum-repulsion geometries, distinguish electron-domain geometry from molecular geometry, understand lone-pair angular asymmetry, and apply predictions to real molecules through two interactive challenges.",
    "workplace": "wp_vsepr_lab",
    "start": "step_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. The Core Idea of VSEPR",
          "description": "A central atom appears before you. Electron pairs \u2014 both bonding pairs and lone pairs \u2014 repel one another and arrange themselves as far apart as possible. VSEPR takes a flat Lewis structure as input and predicts the real 3D shape. Study the water Lewis structure in the reference panel: each single, double, or triple bond, and each lone pair, counts as ONE electron domain. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_intro_narration",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_geometry_name",
              "poi": "poi_top",
              "text": "Central Atom"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lewis_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_lewis_water_image",
              "poi": "poi_lewis_display"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_central_atom",
              "type": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lewis_panel",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_repulsion"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_repulsion",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. Minimum-Repulsion Principle",
          "description": "Watch electron domains push apart. Two domains settle 180\u00b0 apart (linear). Add a third and they form trigonal planar at 120\u00b0. A fourth produces a tetrahedron at 109.5\u00b0. Faint repulsion vectors flash between domains as they rearrange, then fade as each geometry stabilizes. Click Next when the tetrahedron has settled."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_repulsion_anim",
              "poi": "poi_domain_field",
              "state": "settle_linear_to_tetrahedral"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_domain_count",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Domains: 2 -> 3 -> 4"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_catalog"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_catalog",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. The Five Electron-Domain Geometries",
          "description": "Building from the tetrahedron, a fifth domain forms trigonal bipyramidal (3 equatorial at 120\u00b0, 2 axial at 90\u00b0). A sixth forms octahedral (all neighbors at 90\u00b0). Rotate the model to view from any angle. The summary panel shows all five geometries with domain counts (2,3,4,5,6) and angles (180\u00b0, 120\u00b0, 109.5\u00b0, 90\u00b0/120\u00b0, 90\u00b0). Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_geometry_catalog_anim",
              "poi": "poi_domain_field",
              "state": "trigonal_bipyramidal_to_octahedral"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_geometry",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_summary_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_geometry_name",
              "poi": "poi_summary_display",
              "text": "Linear 180 | Trigonal Planar 120 | Tetrahedral 109.5 | Trig. Bipyramidal 90/120 | Octahedral 90"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_summary_panel",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_edg_vs_mg"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_edg_vs_mg",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "4. Electron-Domain vs. Molecular Geometry",
          "description": "The tetrahedral arrangement returns as methane (CH4): all four domains bonding, so both geometries are tetrahedral. Replace one bond with a lone pair (NH3): electron-domain geometry stays tetrahedral, but molecular geometry becomes trigonal pyramidal. Replace a second bond (H2O): molecular geometry becomes bent. The underlying tetrahedron never changed \u2014 lone pairs simply aren't 'seen' in the molecular shape. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_edg_vs_mg_anim",
              "poi": "poi_model_center",
              "state": "ch4_to_nh3_to_h2o"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_mg",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_geometry_name",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "EDG: Tetrahedral | MG: Tetrahedral -> Trig. Pyramidal -> Bent"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_lonepair"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_lonepair",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "5. Lone Pair Angular Asymmetry",
          "description": "Methane (109.5\u00b0), ammonia (107\u00b0), and water (104.5\u00b0) appear side by side with bond angles labeled. The lone-pair clouds are drawn larger \u2014 they are held by only one nucleus, so they spread out more and compress the remaining bonding angles. Toggle the overlay to compare how the H-X-H angle shrinks as lone pairs are added. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_lonepair_size_anim",
              "poi": "poi_model_center",
              "state": "compare_ch4_nh3_h2o"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_lonepair",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_geometry_name",
              "poi": "poi_model_center",
              "text": "CH4 109.5  |  NH3 107  |  H2O 104.5"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_molecule_model",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_gallery"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_gallery",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "6. Gallery of Real Molecules",
          "description": "Approach each molecule to see its Lewis structure beside its 3D structure: CO2 (linear), BF3 (trigonal planar), CH4 (tetrahedral), PF5 (trigonal bipyramidal), SF6 (octahedral), plus lone-pair examples NH3 (trigonal pyramidal), H2O (bent), and XeF4 (square planar). Confirm the count of bonding domains and lone pairs on each central atom. When you have detected the gallery zone, proceed."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_gallery",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_gallery_molecules_anim",
              "poi": "poi_gallery_row",
              "state": "all_eight_molecules"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_gallery",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_gallery",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_gallery",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_geometry_name",
              "poi": "poi_gallery_row",
              "text": "CO2 | BF3 | CH4 | PF5 | SF6 | NH3 | H2O | XeF4"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_gallery",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "det_molecule_gallery_anchor"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_drag_challenge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_drag_challenge",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "7. Challenge: Drag the Domains",
          "description": "A central atom appears with electron domains floating loosely around it. Drag each domain into the correct minimum-repulsion arrangement. When the angles are within tolerance, the configuration snaps into place. Repeat for 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 domains, then for mixed cases \u2014 for example four domains where one is a lone pair, which still sits in a tetrahedral position while producing a trigonal pyramidal molecular shape. Click Next when finished."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_drag_challenge_anim",
              "poi": "poi_domain_field",
              "state": "interactive_drag"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_domain_count",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Drag domains to the correct geometry"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_electron_domains",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_pick_challenge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_pick_challenge",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "8. Challenge: Pick the Structure",
          "description": "A Lewis structure of an unfamiliar molecule appears (e.g. SF4, ClF3, BrF5, or I3-) beside three or four candidate 3D structures. Determine the steric number and lone-pair count, then select the matching geometry. Each answer is confirmed or corrected with reasoning: count domains, name the electron-domain geometry, account for lone pairs, name the molecular geometry. Repeat across several molecules. Click Next to finish."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_lewis_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_pick_challenge_image",
              "poi": "poi_lewis_display"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lewis_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_geometry_name",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Select the correct 3D geometry"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_lewis_panel",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_recap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "9. Recap",
          "description": "Review the five concept areas covered: (1) the minimum-repulsion principle, (2) the five electron-domain geometries and their angles, (3) the distinction between electron-domain and molecular geometry, (4) lone pair angular asymmetry, and (5) the application to real molecules. Each area is highlighted as the narration reviews it. Great work \u2014 you can now predict molecular shape with VSEPR."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_summary_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_summary_image",
              "poi": "poi_summary_display"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_summary_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_recap",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_summary_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_geometry_name",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "VSEPR Complete: 5 Concepts Mastered"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
